    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear focus on exploiting porous pitching matchups to compensate for a high-difficulty encounter with Skenes. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (81.25) stands out as our premier play; his matchup against Fedde is a statistical goldmine, bolstered by a 19% boost from the opposing SP\u2019s xERA and a 4% efficiency bonus derived from Fedde\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a high-probability environment for **Pete Crow-Armstrong** to secure a stolen base. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** (71.68) remains a high-floor anchor in the outfield, benefiting from a favorable platoon advantage and another target-rich opportunity to swipe a bag against a slow-moving Ober. While **Junior Caminero** (63.34) and **Carson Benge** (63.27) lack the same stolen base upside, their strong positional order and relatively soft matchups against P\u00e9rez and Rodriguez respectively make them reliable engines for R and RBI production today.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Chase Meidroth**, whose 49.85 efficiency score was respectable but ultimately insufficient to crack a lineup crowded with high-ceiling platoon options. While **Chase Meidroth** offered a stable baseline, he fell short of the superior volume-adjusted projections offered by **Hyeseong Kim** (61.65), whose explosive 19% SP skill bonus against Rodriguez makes him a better play despite the lower batting order slot. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **Caleb Durbin** and **Kazuma Okamoto**; both remain sidelined, leaving them at a 0.00 score and outside our active considerations.\n\nRisk management remains paramount today, particularly regarding our exposure to Skenes. Both **Trea Turner** (48.87) and **Kyle Schwarber** (47.62) are starting despite facing an elite arm, though their scores are heavily suppressed by a 19% penalty due to the opponent's SP skill. **Trea Turner** is a hold-your-nose start strictly for his leadoff volume and speed, but owners should be prepared for a potential floor-level performance given the high-velocity challenge. Additionally, the wind-in conditions at **Colt Keith**\u2019s park have shaved 5% off his score, capping his power potential against Gausman, but his favorable #3 spot keeps him inside the starting rotation for the time being.",
